iabetes neuropathy pattern", "Systemic infection/viral syndrome: mild fever (37.5°C) with no focal sepsis signs"], "alarm_features": ["Unintentional 5.5kg weight loss (>5% over 1 month)", "Tobacco use 40 pack-years", "Hypertension (177/108 mmHg)", "Anabolic steroid usage"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Brachial Plexopathy (Traumatic/Latrogenic)", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.32, "posterior": 0.32, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.4, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism"}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Thoracic Pulmonary Neoplasm", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.24, "posterior": 0.24, "danger": 0.75, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 3.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.6, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism"}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Cerebrovascular Disease (Subacute Ischemic/Inflammatory)", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.18, "posterior": 0.18, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.5, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism"}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Vascular Compression Syndrome (Subclavian Steal / Occlusion)", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.15, "posterior": 0.15, "danger": 0.5, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.75, "interrupt_relevance": 0.4, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism"}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Musculoskeletal Compression Syndrome (Scalenus Anticus, etc.)", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.09, "danger": 0.3, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}}, "frontier": ["B1", "B2", "B3", "B4"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [], "differential_history": [], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "The patient’s symptoms are subacute (weeks duration) with no immediate life-threatening instability markers. While brachial plexopathy (B) and apical lung tumor (A) are plausible, features like airway patency, stable vitals, and absence of tension pneumothorax, stroke, septic shock, or hemorrhagic compromise do not meet universal instability criteria.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": ""}, "turn_budget_used": 0, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.0, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.0, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Apical lung tumor"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Brachial plexopathy"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Cerebral infarction"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Scalenus anticus syndrome"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Subclavian steal syndrome"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "set()", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1", "label": "Brachial Plexopathy (Traumatic/Latrogenic)", "posterior": 0.32, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [], "considered_alternatives": ["B2", "B3", "B4"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=100%, source=both]\n\nBrachial Plexopathy (Traumatic/Latrogenic) vs Thoracic Pulmonary Neoplasm:\n  Favours first:  burning sensation, deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, electric shock-like sensation\n  Favours second: abnormality of the scrotum, abnormality of tibia morphology, aplasia/hypoplasia of the breasts, hemivertebrae, hypermelanotic macule\n\nBrachial Plexopathy (Traumatic/Latrogenic) vs Cerebrovascular Disease (Subacute Ischemic/Inflammatory):\n  Favours first:  burning sensation, deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, electric shock-like sensation\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cornea morphology, abnormal cutaneous elastic fiber morphology, abnormal facial shape\n\nBrachial Plexopathy (Traumatic/Latrogenic) vs Vascular Compression Syndrome (Subclavian Steal / Occlusion):\n  Favours first:  burning sensation, deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, electric shock-like sensation\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n\nBrachial Plexopathy (Traumatic/Latrogenic) vs Musculoskeletal Compression Syndrome (Scalenus Anticus, etc.):\n  Favours first:  burning sensation, deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, electric shock-like sensation\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n\nThoracic Pulmonary Neoplasm vs Cerebrovascular Disease (Subacute Ischemic/Inflammatory):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the scrotum, abnormality of tibia morphology, aplasia/hypoplasia of the breasts, hemivertebrae, hypermelanotic macule\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cornea morphology, abnormal cutaneous elastic fiber morphology, abnormal facial shape\n\nThoracic Pulmonary Neoplasm vs Vascular Compression Syndrome (Subclavian Steal / Occlusion):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the scrotum, abnormality of tibia morphology, aplasia/hypoplasia of the breasts, hemivertebrae, hypermelanotic macule\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n\nThoracic Pulmonary Neoplasm vs Musculoskeletal Compression Syndrome (Scalenus Anticus, etc.):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the scrotum, abnormality of tibia morphology, aplasia/hypoplasia of the breasts, hemivertebrae, hypermelanotic macule\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n\nCerebrovascular Disease (Subacute Ischemic/Inflammatory) vs Vascular Compression Syndrome (Subclavian Steal / Occlusion):\n  Favours first:  abnormal bleeding, abnormal cornea morphology, abnormal cutaneous elastic fiber morphology, abnormal isoelectric focusing of serum transferrin, abnormal skull morphology\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal cortical gyration\n\nCerebrovascular Disease (Subacute Ischemic/Inflammatory) vs Musculoskeletal Compression Syndrome (Scalenus Anticus, etc.):\n  Favours first:  abnormal bleeding, abnormal cornea morphology, abnormal cutaneous elastic fiber morphology, abnormal isoelectric focusing of serum transferrin, abnormal skull morphology\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal cortical gyration\n\nRelated to Thoracic Pulmonary Neoplasm: benign axillary neoplasm, benign neoplasm, benign neoplasm of chest wall\n\nRelated to Cerebrovascular Disease (Subacute Ischemic/Inflammatory): angiodysplasia, arterial calcification of infancy, arterial disorder\n\nRelated to Vascular Compression Syndrome (Subclavian Steal / Occlusion): carpal tunnel syndrome, meralgia paresthetica, peripheral neuropathy\n\nRelated to Musculoskeletal Compression Syndrome (Scalenus Anticus, etc.): carpal tunnel syndrome, meralgia paresthetica, peripheral neuropathy\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 55-year-old male bodybuilder presents to the emergency department with weakness of his right arm → upper limb asymmetry → Thoracic Pulmonary Neoplasm\n  A 55-year-old male bodybuilder presents to the emergency department with weakness of his right arm → hyporeflexia of upper limbs → Vascular Compression Syndrome (Subclavian Steal / Occlusion)\n  A 55-year-old male bodybuilder presents to the emergency department with weakness of his right arm → hyporeflexia of upper limbs → Musculoskeletal Compression Syndrome (Scalenus Anticus, etc.)"}
